      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Journal of Shoulder and Elbow Surgery Board of Trustees. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Lateral epicondylitis is a common condition encountered by orthopedic surgeons. Whereas the majority of patients improve with conservative management, a small percentage will require surgery. The purpose of this study was to compare the clinical outcomes of surgical débridement of the common extensor origin alone with débridement combined with rotation of an anconeus muscle flap in patients who failed to respond to conservative management of chronic lateral epicondylitis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Fifty-seven patients who failed to respond to a minimum of 5 months of conservative treatment for lateral epicondylitis were retrospectively reviewed. Patients in group 1 were treated with open débridement of the common extensor origin. Patients in group 2 were treated with open débridement combined with rotation of an anconeus muscle flap. Outcome measures included elbow range of motion, grip strength, visual analog scale (VAS) for pain score, and Disabilities of the Arm, Shoulder, and Hand (DASH) score. Statistical analyses were performed by Student's t test with 95% confidence intervals."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "At final follow-up, average DASH scores were significantly lower in group 2. There were no significant differences between the groups with regard to elbow range of motion or grip strength. VAS pain scores were significantly reduced in both groups. Preoperative VAS pain scores were significantly greater in group 2; however, at final follow-up, there was no significant difference between groups. There were no apparent complications in either group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "In addition to débridement of the common extensor origin, the rotation of an anconeus muscle flap may improve outcomes in cases of lateral epicondylitis that require operative intervention."
          }
        ]
      },
